---
/**
 * The platform's wordmark: a drawn `D` followed by "ezabin" in the site's own
 * font. One component, so the logo can never drift between the header, the auth
 * pages and whatever comes next.
 *
 * WHY THE D IS A DRAWING AND NOT A LETTER (CURRENT_TASK סשן ב׳, 2026-08-05).
 * It is the right half of an octagon: a straight back, and a bowl made of three
 * cut edges. Everything about it is matched to the wordmark's own font by
 * MEASUREMENT rather than by eye — the numbers below were read off the font by
 * rendering it to a canvas and scanning pixels, because two earlier attempts at
 * "looks about right" were both visibly wrong (too light, then too narrow).
 *
 * THE DENSITY IS THE POINT — 800 AT -0.04em (owner, סשן ג׳, 2026-08-05).
 * Heavy weight plus negative tracking is what closes the seven letters into ONE
 * SOLID SHAPE, and that shape is the logo. This is worth stating because the
 * opposite was tried first, the same day: the wordmark went to 600 at zero
 * tracking on the theory that 700 read as "bold UI text", it shipped, and the
 * owner's verdict was that it had become *cheaper* — "לפחות קודם זה היה גוש
 * אחיד". It was reverted within the hour. Opening a wordmark up reads as thin,
 * and thin reads as cheap. **Do not lighten or open this again.** What moved
 * instead is the axis he actually asked about: one weight heavier and one step
 * tighter, so the block is denser than it was.
 *
 * IT STANDS TO THE ASCENDER (0.75em), NOT TO THE CAP LINE (owner, סשן ג׳: "רואים
 * בבירור שה-D יותר נמוכה ורחבה"). He was right, and it is measurable. In Heebo
 * 800 the ink tops are: b 0.75em, i 0.745em, D 0.71em. A capital is SHORTER than
 * the ascenders around it — normal in running text, where a capital reads as a
 * capital. Here the mark is a flat-topped slab and the deficit reads as squat
 * instead, and it is the widest single glyph in the word (0.5525em against the
 * b's 0.485), so "low and wide" was a literal description of a 0.04em gap.
 *
 * The fix is height only. The ink is NOT wider or narrower than before — the
 * width/height ratio drops from 0.78 to 0.74 because the height moved, which is
 * the whole trick: nothing about the letter's mass changed, it simply stopped
 * being the short one. The stem is held at its measured ABSOLUTE weight while
 * the box grows, or a taller mark would silently become a heavier one.
 *
 * The bottom stays ON the baseline. Round letters (e, a, o) overshoot 0.0075em
 * below it and flat ones do not; that asymmetry is the font's, and copying the
 * overshoot onto a flat edge would make the mark hang.
 *
 * EVERY OTHER NUMBER FOLLOWS THE WEIGHT, because they are all Heebo's own,
 * re-measured at 800 (the same scan reproduces the previous figures exactly at
 * 700, which is what says it is the method that produced them). In this viewBox
 * 28 units = 0.75em, so a unit is 0.02679em:
 *
 *   height            28 units = 0.75em — Heebo 800's ascender, not its cap
 *   back (vertical)   6.160 = 0.165em — Heebo 800's stem, unchanged in absolute
 *                             terms (it was 6.507 units when a unit was smaller)
 *   bowl's right side 6.160 — a straight edge beside a straight edge; a lighter
 *                             one reads as a mistake rather than as contrast
 *   diagonals         5.567 = 0.9037 of the stem — a diagonal looks lighter than
 *                             its measured width
 *   horizontals       4.922 = 0.799 of the stem — a horizontal looks heavier
 *   letter width      20.626 = 0.5525em, exactly Heebo 800's own D — unchanged
 *   octagon cut       8.2 = 28/(2+√2), tied to the HEIGHT, which is what keeps
 *                             the silhouette half of a REGULAR octagon
 *
 * The two optical ratios (0.799 and 0.9037) are deliberately CARRIED OVER from
 * the 700 drawing rather than re-measured: they correct for how an eye reads a
 * horizontal and a diagonal, which is not a fact about a weight. The stem and
 * the width are the only measured inputs.
 *
 * THE `-0.0163em` BETWEEN THE D AND THE `e` is derived, not chosen:
 *   margin = the font's own D right-side-bearing + the tracking - the SVG's padding
 *          = 0.03711 + (-0.04) - 0.01339
 * (the padding term is 0.5 units, so it moved when the unit did)
 * The bearing is Heebo 800's; the tracking is there because every OTHER pair in
 * the word gets it, so a mark that skipped it would sit looser than the letters
 * around it; the padding is the 0.5 units this viewBox carries outside the ink.
 * A mark spaced like a symbol instead of like a letter is what made early
 * versions read as "an icon glued next to a word".
 *
 * COLOUR. On a light surface the letters carry the site's brand gradient — the
 * one `.btn` uses, unchanged — as ONE ramp across the whole wordmark rather than
 * per letter. The D covers the first 16.7% of the wordmark's width, so it gets
 * exactly that slice (#2a3c40 → #2d4045) and the text continues from there.
 * Giving the D its own full gradient makes it end light and the `e` restart
 * dark, which reads as two pieces stuck together.
 *
 * Height is a hard-coded `0.75em`, not a font-relative unit like `1cap` or
 * `1ex`: Heebo's metrics are known and measured here, and hard-coding them
 * means the mark can't shift if the font fails to load and a fallback with
 * different metrics paints for a frame.
 *
 * ACCESSIBILITY. The `D` is a drawing and the rest is text, so the DOM says
 * "ezabin" — a screen reader, and anything else reading the accessible name,
 * would announce the brand without its first letter. The whole lockup is
 * therefore one `role="img"` with the real name on it, and everything inside is
 * hidden from the tree. The visible text stays real text (it is what gives the
 * mark the font's own kerning and what keeps it sharp at any zoom); it is simply
 * not what the name is read from.
 */
interface Props {
  /** `brand` = the site gradient inside the letters (light surfaces).
   *  `white` = solid white, for a brand-coloured surface. */
  tone?: 'brand' | 'white';
  /** Any CSS length. Everything else is in `em`, so this is the only size knob;
   *  omit it to inherit (the header sizes the logo through `.logo`). */
  size?: string;
  /** Adds the Hebrew line under the name. Off by default: in a header or a form
   *  it would be a second voice in a place that has room for one. */
  tagline?: boolean;
  class?: string;
}

const { tone = 'brand', size, tagline = false, class: className = '' } = Astro.props;

/** The accessible name. Not `store.name` from the config: this component draws
 *  these exact seven letters, so the two must not be able to disagree. */
const NAME = 'Dezabin';
const TAGLINE = 'מתחם חנויות דיגיטלי';

const toneClass =
  tone === 'white'
    ? 'text-white'
    : 'bg-[linear-gradient(135deg,#2a3c40,#3a5260)] bg-clip-text text-transparent';
---

<span
  role="img"
  aria-label={tagline ? `${NAME} — ${TAGLINE}` : NAME}
  class:list={[
    // `dz-logo` carries no styling — it is a stable hook, because the rest of
    // this class list is utilities and a stylesheet cannot address those. The
    // header needs to nudge THIS element and nothing else near it.
    'dz-logo',
    'inline-flex flex-col items-start gap-[0.05em] align-middle',
    toneClass,
    className,
  ]}
  style={size ? `font-size:${size}` : undefined}
>
  <span dir="ltr" class="inline-flex items-baseline font-extrabold leading-none whitespace-nowrap tracking-[-0.04em] text-[1em]">
  {/* `max-w-none` is not decoration: reset.css sets `svg { max-width:100% }`,
      which clamps a fixed-size inline SVG inside a narrow box — the same trap
      that shrank icons in the products table. `shrink-0` does not cover it,
      because max-width is not flex shrinking. */}
  <svg
    class="h-[0.75em] w-auto max-w-none shrink-0 block me-[-0.0163em]"
    viewBox="10.75 8 21.626 28"
    aria-hidden="true"
    focusable="false"
  >
    {tone === 'brand' && (
      <defs>
        {/* The D's slice of the shared ramp — see the header comment. */}
        <linearGradient id="dz-d-grad" x1="0" y1="0" x2="1" y2="1">
          <stop offset="0" stop-color="#2a3c40" />
          <stop offset="1" stop-color="#2d4045" />
        </linearGradient>
      </defs>
    )}
    <path
      fill={tone === 'white' ? '#fff' : 'url(#dz-d-grad)'}
      fill-rule="evenodd"
      d="M11.25 8 L23.676 8 L31.876 16.2 L31.876 27.8 L23.676 36 L11.25 36 Z
         M17.410 12.922 L20.726 12.922 L25.716 17.912 L25.716 26.088 L20.726 31.078 L17.410 31.078 Z"
    />
    </svg><span class="text-[1em] leading-none" aria-hidden="true">ezabin</span>
  </span>
  {tagline && (
    /* HEEBO, AT ITS NATURAL SPACING — both halves of that are the fix (2026-08-05).
     *
     * It was Rubik 600 stretched with +0.0356em of tracking to reach the name's
     * width. Two problems, one cause. Hebrew is drawn to sit close: tracking it
     * open does not enlarge it, it loosens it, and a loosened Hebrew word under a
     * tight Latin wordmark is what reads as amateur (owner: "לא מספיק מקצועי ולא
     * מספיק מתאים לאתר"). And a second family for one line on one page fails the
     * first design-line test — is it already in the system — while costing an
     * eleventh font preload on EVERY page and a second face that `font-display:
     * optional` can refuse at first paint. Rubik and Heebo are siblings anyway
     * (Heebo is Rubik's Latin with Hebrew drawn for it), so the family was buying
     * almost no difference for all of that.
     *
     * The width match is kept — it is what makes this a lockup rather than two
     * stacked lines — and it is solved with SIZE at the font's own spacing.
     *
     * FLUSH ON BOTH ENDS, INK TO INK, AND THAT NEEDS THE MARGIN BELOW (owner,
     * סשן ג׳, 2026-08-05: "חשוב שהטקסט בעברית — כולל הצ׳ופצ׳יק של הי׳ — ישב בול
     * על הרוחב"). Matching the two ink WIDTHS is only half the job, and it is the
     * half that was done before. The other half is that a line box is not its
     * ink: Heebo 500 leaves 0.0725em of side bearing to the right of the מ, while
     * the wordmark leaves only 0.0075em to the right of the n. Align the boxes —
     * which is what a flex column with `items-start` does on its own — and the
     * Hebrew therefore starts a visible pixel INSIDE the English at the hero's
     * size, every time, no matter how exact the width ratio is. Both numbers were
     * read by rendering each line and scanning the pixels; the margin cancels the
     * difference:
     *
     *   margin-inline-start = (the wordmark's 0.0075 - 0.0725 x 0.3886) / 0.3886
     *
     * The division is because `em` in this span resolves against the span's OWN
     * font-size. In the lockup's em it is only -0.0206em, so the line hangs that
     * far past the lockup's right edge on purpose — that overhang IS the fix.
     *
     * 0.3886em is the SIZE, and it is the arithmetic ratio plus a correction that
     * only a real build could give. The ratio first: the wordmark's ink is
     * 3.2925em (the D's 0.5525 + a 0.0303 gap + "ezabin" at 2.71 with the
     * tracking already in it) and Heebo 500's own "מתחם חנויות דיגיטלי" is
     * 8.4825em, so 3.2925/8.4825 = 0.3882. Shipped at 0.3882 the Hebrew still
     * ended a pixel INSIDE the D on every desktop viewport — sub-pixel rounding
     * of a 19-glyph run, which no amount of arithmetic predicts. So the last step
     * was a bisection against the built site, scanning the rendered pixels of the
     * hero at 11 viewports x DPR 1/2/3: 0.3899 crossed OUTSIDE by a pixel, 0.3882
     * sat one inside, 0.3886 is the crossover.
     *
     * WHAT IT ACHIEVES, measured, and this is the bar to beat if anyone changes
     * it: the right edge is EXACTLY flush in all 33 combinations, and the left is
     * 0 on desktop at DPR 1 and never more than one device pixel anywhere. That
     * is the floor — `text-align:justify` on a fixed width was built and measured
     * as an alternative and did not beat it, at the cost of a fixed width that
     * would overflow if the Hebrew face ever fell back.
     *
     * RE-SOLVE ALL THREE (size, margin, and the D) if the name's weight, its
     * tracking, or the slogan's text ever change — they are one calculation.
     *
     * Trailing zeros are not decorative here: Tailwind emits no rule at all for
     * `text-[0.390em]`, so the class silently does nothing and the line renders at
     * the inherited size. Never end one of these values in a zero.
     *
     * Weight 500 under an 800: the two-step gap is what keeps this a subtitle
     * rather than a second name. */
    <span
      dir="rtl"
      class="font-medium text-[0.3886em] leading-none ms-[-0.0532em]"
      aria-hidden="true"
    >{TAGLINE}</span>
  )}
</span>
